          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Urine marking may function to delineate territory, aggressive intent, or reproductive communication between cats or as a response to anxiety.",
              "pdf_page": 952,
              "printed_page": 870,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Likelihood of urine marking increases with density of cats in the household; however, urine marking may occur in households with only 1 or 2 cats.",
              "pdf_page": 952,
              "printed_page": 870,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Marking associated with social conflict between cats in the same household, introduction of new cats, new furniture, or novel objects.",
              "pdf_page": 952,
              "printed_page": 870,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Roaming is a normal exploratory behavior for enrichment, food, or sexual activity.",
              "pdf_page": 952,
              "printed_page": 870,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Roaming is more likely in outdoor cats being housed indoors, intact male cats, and cats with a barren indoor environment (resulting in increased motivation to explore outdoors).",
              "pdf_page": 952,
              "printed_page": 870,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "subheading",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Marking",
